      "side": 1,
      "message": "1. Platform Support\nThe code targets the x86 architecture on both Windows and Linux. I\u0027ve tested it on AMD Strix and Intel Lunar Lake systems (Windows 11 and Ubuntu 24.04.2 LTS), as well as on a Qualcomm ARM-based processor running Windows 11.\n\t2. Compiler Details\nI\u0027m using MSVC for Windows builds.\n\t• On Windows x86 machines, I build with the following command:\ncmake .. -G \"Visual Studio 17 2022\" -A x64 -DENABLE_TESTS\u003d1\n\t• I\u0027m using Visual Studio 2022 Pro with the MSVC v143 – VS 2022 C++ x64/x86 build tools installed.\nYou’re correct — MSVC does not define __x86_64__ as a predefined macro. Instead, _M_X64 should be used to detect x64 targets when compiling with MSVC.\nRegarding the macro checks, the condition #if defined(__x86_64__) || defined(_M_X86) is used to identify x86 processors across both Windows and Linux, covering both AMD and Intel platforms. However, you’re right — for MSVC x64 builds, _M_X64 should be used instead of _M_X86.",
      "parentUuid": "bc2a6200_7620df77",
      "revId": "c2d79f2225c02628526b84fec8c84875d8eed4c7",
      "serverId": "e5514cf8-2d6e-3e29-adb4-24cd6dde4bf0"
    },
